Chapter: TrustAnchors
  cites Encode chapter X509
  cites Encode chapter X509Chain
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 THIS IS NOT A ROOT PROGRAM AND MUST NOT GROW INTO ONE. Mozilla, Apple,
 Microsoft and Chrome each ship one; they disagree with each other, they
 run to well over a hundred authorities, and every one of those
 authorities can issue a certificate for any name in the world. Adopting
 such a list would import precisely the trust model the founding document
 sets out to replace: trust as a vendor's list rather than as something
 we can state and defend. So this is seven roots, chosen deliberately,
 each written down with why.

 Damian chose the two issuers, 2026-07-26: DigiCert and Let's Encrypt.
 Between them they cover the software world Codex has to reach during the
 transition -- package indexes, source forges, distribution mirrors -- and
 measurement bears it out: of twelve significant sites sampled that day,
 the visible roots concentrated onto a handful, with Let's Encrypt the
 single most common.

 Every root below was taken from TWO independent distribution paths and
 compared byte for byte, and both paths agreed exactly for all seven. That
 is not proof of authenticity -- nothing short of a physical channel is --
 but it does mean a single compromised path did not put these bytes here.

 For six of them the pair is the Windows root store on the build machine
 and the issuer's own published copy fetched over the network. ISRG Root X2
 is the exception and the exception is worth stating rather than glossing:
 it is not in that store at all, so its second path is the Mozilla NSS
 bundle -- a different DISTRIBUTOR, not a second mirror of the issuer. A
 pair of paths that share an origin is one path wearing two names, which is
 the failure this doctrine exists to prevent, so which two paths were used
 is part of the claim and is recorded per root.

 The last two went in when P-384 did, on 2026-07-28. DigiCert Global Root
 G3 and ISRG Root X2 are the elliptic-curve roots of the same two issuers,
 and they were held out for a reason that no longer holds: this tree
 verified ECDSA only on P-256, both roots are P-384, X509 typed their keys
 KeyEcdsaOther, and x509-can-verify answered False for either. An anchor
 that can never match is worse than a missing one, because it reads as
 coverage. P-384 verification landed, so they are admissible now, and both
 were re-confirmed P-384 and self-signed by reading the certificates rather
 than by trusting their names.

 We say:

Section: The Roots

