Section: Building The Anchor Set

 An anchor is a name and a key, taken from a certificate we hold rather
 than from one a peer sent. The roots are parsed here rather than stored
 pre-split so that the bytes in this file are exactly the bytes the
 issuer published, and can be diffed against them.

 CALL THIS ONCE AND KEEP THE RESULT. A list constant is rebuilt at every
 mention -- that is a documented and measured property of this language,
 not a quirk of this chapter -- so a caller that mentions ta-anchors per
 verification re-parses seven certificates every time.

  ta-anchor-ders : Integer -> List (List Integer)
  ta-anchor-ders (x) = [ta-isrg-x1, ta-dc-global, ta-dc-g2, ta-dc-g4, ta-dc-ev, ta-dc-g3, ta-isrg-x2]

  ta-anchors : Integer -> List X509Anchor
  ta-anchors (x) = ta-collect (ta-anchor-ders 0) 0 []

  ta-collect : List (List Integer), Integer, List X509Anchor -> List X509Anchor
  ta-collect (ders) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length ders then acc
    else ta-add (x509-parse (list-at ders i)) ders i acc

 A root that does not parse is DROPPED, and the count is how a caller
 finds out. ta-anchor-count says how many were supplied and
 list-length (ta-anchors 0) says how many survived; if those two numbers
 differ, an anchor is missing and every chain that needed it will fail
 with ChainNoAnchor rather than with anything that names the cause.
 codex/test/web-chain compares them for exactly that reason.

  ta-anchor-count : Integer -> Integer
  ta-anchor-count (x) = list-length (ta-anchor-ders 0)

  ta-add : Maybe X509Cert, List (List Integer), Integer, List X509Anchor -> List X509Anchor
  ta-add (m) (ders) (i) (acc) =
    when m
      is None -> ta-collect ders (i + 1) acc
      is Just (c) -> ta-collect ders (i + 1) (list-push acc (X509Anchor { xa-subject = c.xc-subject, xa-key = c.xc-key }))

